.SS Introduction
\f[CR]ssl\-tls\-verify\f[R] is a simple cross\-platform command line
tool to test and verify SSL/TLS connections with detailed certificate
inspection.
It supports both direct TLS connections (like HTTPS) and StartTLS
protocol upgrades (like SMTP).
The tool provides comprehensive certificate chain analysis with
pretty\-printed output, making it ideal for debugging SSL/TLS issues,
certificate validation problems, and connection troubleshooting.
.PP
Unlike basic tools like \f[CR]openssl s_client\f[R], this utility
focuses on providing human\-readable certificate information with expiry
warnings, fingerprint validation, and detailed TLS connection state
analysis.
All the certificate parsing and validation is handled by \c
.UR https://go.dev/
go
.UE \c
\(cqs \f[CR]crypto/tls\f[R] and \f[CR]crypto/x509\f[R] packages.
.PP
If you have any question, request or suggestion, please enter it in the
\c
.UR https://github.com/muquit/ssl-tls-verify/issues
Issues
.UE \c
\ with appropriate label.
.SS Background
There was a lingering bug in \c
.UR https://github.com/muquit/mailsend-go
mailsend\-go
.UE \c
\ that \f[CR]\-verifyCert\f[R] was broken (Bug #71) I was not getting
time to look at the code and figure out the issue, so I asked \c
.UR https://claude.ai
Claude AI Sonnet 4.5
.UE \c
\ to write a simple CLI tool in \c
.UR https://go.dev/
go
.UE \c
\ to verify certificates from connection.
Hence, \f[CR]ssl\-tls\-verify\f[R] was born.
After creating this tool, I realized I had missed \f[CR]ServerName\f[R]
in \f[CR]tls.Config\f[R].
.PP
I also love this tool now, it\(cqs much nicer than openssl with its
human\-readable output, clear certificate chain display, and intuitive
formatting.
What started as a debugging utility has become my go\-to tool for
SSL/TLS certificate inspection.
.PP
Hope you find the tool useful as well.
